# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# The same two windows, from a process of its own.  Out here the id is
# not a pointer the shell can follow: it rides the wire on every
# message, and a session that was ITSELF a window quietly became the
# second one and wore its name.  The application is started the way an
# installed application is started -- through the runner, from the
# Applications menu -- so nothing about the road is special.
wait 25
click 980 30
wait 4
menu-pick Applications
wait 4
expect-menu Twowin
menu-pick Twowin
wait 150
note two windows over the wire, each wearing its own name
click-window TwoAlpha
wait 8
expect-title TwoAlpha
click-window TwoBeta
wait 8
expect-title TwoBeta
note keys reach the window they were typed into, over the wire
key x
key x
wait 20
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_twowin/beta 2
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_twowin/alpha 0
click-window TwoAlpha
wait 8
key x
wait 20
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_twowin/alpha 1
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_twowin/beta 2
note the strip carries the menus of the window in front, from the
note description that came down the wire for THAT window
click 60 8
wait 8
expect-menu Alpha typed 1
menu-pick Alpha typed 1
wait 8
note and the OTHER window's rows are its own: a wire that dropped the
note id would show these two windows the same menu
click-window TwoBeta
wait 8
click 60 8
wait 8
expect-menu Beta typed 2
menu-pick Beta typed 2
wait 8
click-window TwoAlpha
wait 8
click 60 8
wait 8
note closing one window leaves the other, and the process behind it:
note the row asks the application, the application gives that window
note back by name, and the name is carried over the wire
menu-pick Close Alpha
wait 30
expect-no-title TwoAlpha
click-window TwoBeta
wait 8
expect-title TwoBeta
key x
wait 20
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_twowin/beta 3
